#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115121 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "7d530adb-8bcc-53f1-b1bf-674be8f7046e" :question/math-academy-id "e-589" :question/problem "![](images/70/70dc1594c0072b83429fc1f5d6be4fa454f6650c3eeef37c41c2d3ec691bb35f.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ from $x=0$ to $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $360^{\\circ}$about the line $y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b0427e7d37245d86d347d6d4f867ae3d17e5f0a030feb53d28ad4bbc856f85c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tV &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\big[\\: \\left({\\color{blue}\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left({\\color{brown}\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\:\\big]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t \t&= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[\\: {\\color{blue}x^2 }- \\left({\\color{brown}-1} \\right)\\:\\right]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]  \n                &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+1)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^4+2x^2+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{x^5}{5}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{3}+x \\right) \\Bigg|_{0}^{1} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{2}{3}+1 \\right] - 0 \\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{28 \\pi}{15}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115122 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "a02ed650-9bbd-552b-9d80-c2d8f1d581b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-590" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e99f6cd10a72c91e5c7430a9ee18a45ba4dc09f941c779077567436a5fe90405.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1},$ the vertical line $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Find an integral expression for the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^3+1\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73fc04f5a8a56b0faba6ec2a77f8aafd864c84786adfe7da9ccda99d0c8a4d8f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=y^3+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{3}+1)-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{({y}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{6}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "413e2100-cec8-5c20-981a-73b9a61ddbc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7148" :question/problem "![](images/40/401368d830825a55e27fc6ba191a1c3dbb80d93b2e0af74db3337523817d2c65.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2,$ for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1,$ as shown above. Find an integral that represents the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "4da6aa8a-844d-53c4-b573-2f81acb5a5c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7149" :question/problem "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{y},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=4,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=2.$\n\n![](images/a5/a56f30e5912629c9854e68d4837a37dae55a005e98ef6cf5379b5eb81022afb7.png)" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =2 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4-4\\sqrt{y}+y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {[4y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(4(4)-\\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2}+\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =\\pi [16-\\frac{64}{3}+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d517681-8ca3-5922-a210-f7d68fbd36ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534356} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb3753a-fee0-5ce8-b33a-4e43e42c5870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534500} {:answer/id #uuid "8f568eb4-6ce0-559e-81d7-859c5216f036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534580} {:answer/id #uuid "9811b230-7ef8-5ebc-8a3f-b70ca6db6be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534604} {:answer/id #uuid "f95b558e-ab41-5c5f-b335-0ae58978c14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95b558e-ab41-5c5f-b335-0ae58978c14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534816} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cfa96a4-e989-5e97-a3e8-83dc73708ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31757f7a-27f2-5cad-90a3-e9168497ad54" :question/math-academy-id "q-120535" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fa5e5b2300c3395d48f5355352cd6ece7477f3bae8e8c185f0f869fa197b0e9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2)+(x-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1821ff500c901d9f072fe56101f17e579018d0710543c4a2e371214894725c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11fc6c5f-f3de-5a82-9ef6-baa130370ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534303} {:answer/id #uuid "8f762836-19b7-5da7-9721-d9209f79d770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534581} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4c31b1-5151-50a1-a311-e847ea06c5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}+2{e}^{y}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534731} {:answer/id #uuid "db1b9e49-00da-5316-bb71-7c891cad6f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-e)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534757} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9ac4d7-eb0c-564c-af55-a27d0dcbfe73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}+2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11fc6c5f-f3de-5a82-9ef6-baa130370ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534303} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de61be3-338b-5af9-988b-fcfbb09b5e3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "059b810f-7faa-5870-a600-8d8d6445cd02" :question/math-academy-id "q-120810" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7eb8812e90e4862a3658773186fe3809b212d58d5d22613fac6d9067ae4ccbc9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f0/f0ddb5b21bdff9cbed426abed5bf9b94c4c4c4d742e2eb90dcdb92895c2adf3a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(e-{e}^{y})^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c716c79-f420-5422-84b3-0336b564a145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534380} {:answer/id #uuid "5f06fca2-f629-5be7-a4a3-1f4853dc03eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1-\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534458} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfeeed5-c339-5e20-92cf-869495d75216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534493} {:answer/id #uuid "cc893279-f68c-5b8a-aa8a-10a3e7137e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{4}{y}+\\frac{4}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534721} {:answer/id #uuid "ef803d29-9d1e-5966-824f-0510361b80aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dfeeed5-c339-5e20-92cf-869495d75216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534493} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3236f7-ef4e-5824-b7ca-2c3a37165b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fe06b9c-2dd8-5751-a283-a97db5419aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120816" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d154a914d58f5b695adec9dd873ac067ba8280a4fefffd4f2f5559ff5ee04da.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{1-x},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\,⟹\\,x=1-\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ to $y=-1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/23/239113c4d2bbca26c694796f6f17bd26bebf47697dad53f7af832088213fd4c9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1-\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{[\\,(2)-(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{(1+\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d86b88-e994-55d8-9aab-8c7074f2fa73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{2}-y+2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534302} {:answer/id #uuid "17ee6b3d-079f-5a5f-87b1-7015ef18baa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+2y+2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534318} {:answer/id #uuid "21d23244-e732-5433-957b-488175409ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534335} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4f38a5-4afe-53b3-8d55-92144694484d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3/2}({y}^{2}+4y+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534540} {:answer/id #uuid "f311bd18-39bc-5966-bcd6-64f303891d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21d23244-e732-5433-957b-488175409ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534335} :answer-field/id #uuid "57561582-2db8-5679-9544-434bd74cb0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27279421-a932-5375-bd1a-4824ccac1d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-120837" :question/problem "![](images/68/68359143b50b4d6187f2d50793b1d3cb4b48656f1996cd222cb5d07f666b535e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=-1-x,$ the vertical line $x=-3,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-3,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=-1-x\\,⟹\\,x=-1-y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=-3$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-y & =-3 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73d5da7e1bd4b409574f6e9ab034dde51012eb956b3ea72a3529f7d432fe781d.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=-1-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-1-y)-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-y)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f86113-90f2-58e5-9e9a-3d5f87a4056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534313} {:answer/id #uuid "15e87c4b-5a56-5f0a-9604-0d35fbeb9425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534316} {:answer/id #uuid "456bc927-97e0-508b-bce6-ec15a7546b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534396} {:answer/id #uuid "7e634175-26e2-5b33-932f-02da2cc68430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534539} {:answer/id #uuid "be4c2add-16e0-5c1d-96be-00048e79502c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e634175-26e2-5b33-932f-02da2cc68430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534539} :answer-field/id #uuid "3324bc9b-8a8d-5b4e-82c5-238fdef233a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2588c644-3085-550f-abbf-182631d5c4e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-120906" :question/problem "![](images/14/144259aa150936de0e3425c13f953da0833d87c2aba1304aa39b3c47fda03550.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1,$ the horizontal line $y=1,$  the horizontal line $y=0,$ and the vertical line $x=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1$, as shown above. Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left function and which is the right, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/8a/8a17502c300fae56e9f2e1706d23a3e5a7d3233eebff9a77958d117a0135adb0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the resulting solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(6\\sqrt{y}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{y}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24\\sqrt{y}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24{y}^{1/2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+24\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}+4y)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(18(1)^{2}+16(1)^{3/2}+4(1))-0] \\\\  & =38\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b40c85-8993-522e-96cc-df161ffe20c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97b40c85-8993-522e-96cc-df161ffe20c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534602} :answer-field/id #uuid "68dd3cc2-e623-50ea-9f8e-1f4fb1b37990" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fd041ce-cfe7-560a-8362-3b96b6047662" :question/math-academy-id "q-352955" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e402d36061211a9e51df852f7fa595eb212e5c6ce9b3d43a2d53f9bea1fedd3.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=(x-1)^{4}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/2a/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }]-[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }]) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400565d9-66a0-529c-8554-38d138f2516a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "400565d9-66a0-529c-8554-38d138f2516a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534387} :answer-field/id #uuid "aada71ef-29a8-53e6-9657-ff4c532d0d90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4017e457-d6e9-5799-a313-b509dcbd530f" :question/math-academy-id "q-353047" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curve $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=5$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ad/ad089d39428498123469a2ac7895f7d04b8b3ff9dac18a1698462bfc7fabae99.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/97/975f68d06bb52e162ad792987899509f4090c03ec1b55af6335982a2951f6bbf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,5-(4-(x-1)^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1+(x-1)^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+2(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-1)^{4}+2(x-1)^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}+2-(-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}+0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{43}{15}+\\frac{13}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f256f46-100c-5687-961a-0d3ebef98f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f256f46-100c-5687-961a-0d3ebef98f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534578} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e380c07-676e-5d1a-a447-464aebaba18c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f10ec35-272f-563f-8af5-2c0fc2e74c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353061" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d8f2d52a0d943a4eeb1d323cc90db145c9b87b57e87d595a7ca5809498e7a6a.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=y+1,$ the vertical line $x=4,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=4.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=4$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =4 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=3$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f9/f9fd1773b9599b1a4b61fc58c854bf998ed147a9707f719a80a3588874c0eb4f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[4-(y+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(3-y)^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-{3}^{3}) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f569a1f0-e362-5804-81cd-4cb22377f499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f569a1f0-e362-5804-81cd-4cb22377f499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534811} :answer-field/id #uuid "02b568fb-76bc-5e15-9c73-e0b3712cc107" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08dd2009-a210-5f29-bbaa-81488a5664f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353068" :question/problem "![](images/54/5453dfc44e11cad377c1e8e2c30fc23d537d4423d38fab4428395362f51d9412.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}+1,$ the vertical line $x=5,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=5.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=5$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/e8/e83c5729ef5db920a0b451a8571ad84f45d2b4e950ab831ced0da2c0a12cea50.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={y}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,5-({y}^{2}+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(16y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc19dac-699d-59b0-87b6-ff3f7c64d409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{25/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534452} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad741f4-7070-5f9a-8539-bd9c8f502b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534484} {:answer/id #uuid "85da4021-495f-522c-b62f-28b0804dabf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534553} {:answer/id #uuid "8607c785-004e-5380-8266-895cff651e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534554} {:answer/id #uuid "be6e9ea7-3b03-5b53-ba44-976064339a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}(4{x}^{4}+4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8607c785-004e-5380-8266-895cff651e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534554} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e290c7-ae9f-5409-9b4f-541a37e1ca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e80b3506-231f-529b-b14d-6bb0a866165f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49890" :question/problem "![](images/28/2817662752008c41b76d1beb9bec73907f55d836524fe48c583997670421f70b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{5}{2}],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e1/e1774466f020515d3296f2e48fda0a61154bddbfd2285a67c97bdc7e35b60da5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(2{x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e395b02-9179-5af5-8f19-5bbe78d139f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534293} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb763f1-9aef-52ca-9fd8-c473b0438b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534686} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b82ef7-2f85-5c83-94fb-8edac9a6b879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534770} {:answer/id #uuid "e8576b89-9011-5d9b-86c1-433e60fde2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534781} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8852ed-05e5-5668-baa9-257a358a079a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b82ef7-2f85-5c83-94fb-8edac9a6b879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534770} :answer-field/id #uuid "10a2ecc1-4bf8-5461-93f0-d493b4fcede2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f36b991-1cba-5c10-9333-13a2ade76e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50007" :question/problem "![](images/84/848b73762d79b0b7b898f3c011e4118256b5fca37fa9d702313bc16da04b4c3d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [0,1],$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b634be23e06f6038e00b91f27eadacd1d1bcc51516d741786cc433a1ce731ce.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e18ec7-b86d-52c4-a800-15588ea1dbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534733} {:answer/id #uuid "d2988696-3e8e-5d85-a5e3-6f62050554f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534737} {:answer/id #uuid "e58d8772-610c-564c-9aa1-9a6eaf4c3487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3})\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534772} {:answer/id #uuid "e76bc17f-0baf-5d2a-9985-1a028ff25f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({y}^{2/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534777} {:answer/id #uuid "ff41c014-8136-5e89-92d6-465efca4119d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff41c014-8136-5e89-92d6-465efca4119d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534824} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2b25ef-5cc5-53ca-8aad-45786fb6f09c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4260f61-3124-52b4-9558-94f62f160010" :question/math-academy-id "q-50017" :question/problem "![](images/29/293593a043a7713d87a2f8ad8a2eaecee26b34288e4662bb4b2fce834e703e56.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ the vertical line $x=-1$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/60/600ef6d7265add5c669a29394e45facae040292ce19caad9dcae390979b604b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt[3]{y})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e479de-ced4-5c94-a17a-d38d70a5d8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534310} {:answer/id #uuid "45a333a3-e039-5463-812c-9bff893827ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{0}(4x-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534397} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2bf703-4e49-50bf-9ab2-b775bfc98742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534651} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdea9f5-56f5-5aa5-9f72-703a22f500d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534723} {:answer/id #uuid "e855cc0b-f431-5b79-a5c7-f010271a1ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{6}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccdea9f5-56f5-5aa5-9f72-703a22f500d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534723} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b2b3c4-71d4-527c-abf3-f3422fc41129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a85d5623-8f4e-5539-b87a-051d9e911395" :question/math-academy-id "q-50020" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d27538da9cf9ebb39324477a9c3e9eabd950b648507ebc616a11d5c8a6054a19.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ for $x\\in [0,2],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/94/94d3f7e76db23c387366024bdd5dbb25f8adddc25d78bb3fc81f5900b4128fcf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,({x}^{3})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115301 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d402001-2d71-5813-9732-fcc950a7ae1b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "887bb682-847d-5497-b082-069e31b4ca18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534562} {:answer/id #uuid "8f35e7c9-23db-5d10-9546-2d16da17d83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534579} {:answer/id #uuid "9e084eaf-fd07-5c31-9a44-9d16ecb72243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534618} {:answer/id #uuid "aa69c2e1-351a-5624-8415-3234bb23b87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534649} {:answer/id #uuid "eeaa6543-a7cf-5a69-8ac8-dcbdbdc1fd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887bb682-847d-5497-b082-069e31b4ca18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534562} :answer-field/id #uuid "01397a07-f9d3-51fc-adc8-145fbda696db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "236b0649-a813-5e30-b589-a9199c88af15" :question/math-academy-id "q-50022" :question/problem "![](images/10/1066e10a5a9860e371ca2895ec39f7cdce4e000d5cca9f3b478f57cd890d647a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ for $x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ and the horizontal line $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4c086c9a3a99609728bc7c53f5a28ac86431e5c37e2b87bcfd3031a32aa470.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{x})-(\\frac{1}{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42ad968e-05d7-5eb6-92aa-c6d4221319a4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090e8a68-e8fa-5bc8-90fd-9cd2d69e4b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534276} {:answer/id #uuid "259de651-fd50-5b82-8e61-2db5fc5258f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}-2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534345} {:answer/id #uuid "63401029-dbe2-52a7-bcb8-3a4927a7cf88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{y}{4}-2\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534465} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdd43ab-c905-5c98-9b3a-08fb054e4d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534531} {:answer/id #uuid "8e437008-9a45-55bd-a385-ef285947ff5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}+2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bdd43ab-c905-5c98-9b3a-08fb054e4d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534531} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f2c76e-9efc-51e9-af76-e42ebea84266" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a978de85-c2b4-579a-92f4-5064fe99c547" :question/math-academy-id "q-50081" :question/problem "![](images/42/421d588cd290294cf6471704f2459c22b00c8b47756b808e9da267a8007792b2.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" :question/worked-solution "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe take the positive sign since $x\\ge 0.$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}} & =2 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=8$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/c7/c762c0e41cf8862535e5241a00149fdc6e409c3f79630a411cff73fcd11759b0.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}-2)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09506fd1-2815-56b2-9895-a8f2fa17df31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534277} {:answer/id #uuid "14edbb70-481d-567e-9815-ccc321d95b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534312} {:answer/id #uuid "97675cbf-0281-5bcd-b979-29754bf68a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534600} {:answer/id #uuid "b5467958-0603-5580-82c7-141e0c309a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534672} {:answer/id #uuid "d641d391-ba5a-5748-974b-53dbc8762112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97675cbf-0281-5bcd-b979-29754bf68a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534600} :answer-field/id #uuid "94050dc1-3fc5-5684-9d2f-0b67b160855c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48038fbf-e0e6-55ac-98d1-e8c48a8b376a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81661" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d0b7f238afe0f6cb82bb8670b9589aa10490b6e776e7f4a991a561aa0ad2245.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-x}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ from $x=0$ to $x=3,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$about the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc1e2cad0969a629e2c2c4a55ce8ac66c16b67fe2984e8b75dc6aa3a5eeace.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{4-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,(\\sqrt{4-x})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}((4-x)-2\\sqrt{4-x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5-x-2\\sqrt{4-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi (5x{|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{4}{3}(4-x)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((15-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(1-8)) \\\\  & =\\pi (15-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7380ba8b-27af-582b-adbb-5535ef77e4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534513} {:answer/id #uuid "79b24dfe-6073-564c-9ec8-d4c228f54ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534525} {:answer/id #uuid "87805274-7994-5b8b-82ff-5ddcf34f2cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534559} {:answer/id #uuid "d61da461-e738-5414-9b19-a2d59208047c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534748} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb25f6e-a8f3-5f13-96fb-d6e19cd7bc06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87805274-7994-5b8b-82ff-5ddcf34f2cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534559} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b052f20-a657-5fb9-97d6-3e43da2ac8be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0334985-4e7c-56a4-ba8b-035c69ca7733" :question/math-academy-id "q-81681" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c21d4794aeedcc70fe037a936724d2708a484d43c457b2d120927f86f1fa6ffc.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x-2$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-2 & =-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e7/e785138362c91f9034d90ee9d923fc818cd299b3ee5d6d25eb1bf40cabc2f2b0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-2x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-2)-({x}^{2}-2x-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([\\frac{1}{5}(2)^{5}-(2)^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}(2)^{3}\\text{ }]-[0]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{5}-16+\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55110426-9b6c-5147-8c18-32fcf01860b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534435} {:answer/id #uuid "6452e493-c57a-5fe5-a1cf-953bef5c9e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi e}{1+4{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534467} {:answer/id #uuid "af3dfa5f-81db-523c-b026-a7f56854f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(16-{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534657} {:answer/id #uuid "bac171fe-ef9e-5229-9b88-446a005d22bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534682} {:answer/id #uuid "f841f37f-0274-5bed-9030-b86472ded932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bac171fe-ef9e-5229-9b88-446a005d22bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534682} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b227d07-d337-5e85-81ba-0cc9d053f147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ffaec2e-3741-5b7e-88f5-bdff517c9f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81786" :question/problem "![](images/46/4643824b623d5325f53407147f086a95073db91be0148381780668c420ec7c45.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={e}^{y},$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=e$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =e \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/93/93fd0875ea2ba1d1d92bb781a61cfb114ac88b7d240cd825b4842f686f7c760f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[e-{e}^{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2y})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{y+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi (({e}^{2}-0)-2({e}^{2}-e)+(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi (-{e}^{2}+2e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4e-{e}^{2}-1)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c1cc6b6-5253-513c-98b0-9a963895eab6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185c9f21-b220-5b11-8796-8a91d31e7570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (5+6e+{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534321} {:answer/id #uuid "93ec2393-d1d4-5510-a6e4-d1bb9678dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534590} {:answer/id #uuid "a721e4f2-439d-5540-be18-aff7f38c48f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(5+6e+{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534638} {:answer/id #uuid "de381ab8-1a56-54a1-85c0-73ccdbfab087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(10+12e+2{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534761} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cc4d97-ff13-5bfb-89cd-cc7ea14e5f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi (5+12e+{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ec2393-d1d4-5510-a6e4-d1bb9678dc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534590} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb55632-1e14-5d4a-90a8-484b3e57cb27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b9b18aa-2c78-5504-8e8f-08d14250da87" :question/math-academy-id "q-85996" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curve $y={e}^{x},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=3$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/72/72b701d2404e1acdcfba6b1994588419afc8246de6df8bf50e93baca0051df6a.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/89/89d6b6d2d65f161353da5fea9175aaf780f0fdc38405b855fbbc033349a484db.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={e}^{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({e}^{x})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(3-{e}^{x})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-6{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(9x-6{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ([9-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\text{ }]-[-6+\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{29}{2}-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f948be97-572c-5e4f-a5fa-1291cefbb4ac" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08767496-af57-5656-a9a1-caa61817cc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534274} {:answer/id #uuid "68ce0533-1775-50fc-a2d1-e656f5328e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534480} {:answer/id #uuid "8281b9a5-0a80-578b-871f-663a9780da04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534549} {:answer/id #uuid "96f4ffa4-c0a8-5dc0-b1e0-7f339689319c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51\\pi }{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534598} {:answer/id #uuid "aa34b084-e5f8-5ab6-9e0d-5461e1ea38c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08767496-af57-5656-a9a1-caa61817cc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534274} :answer-field/id #uuid "27369d98-76f7-5b69-8322-ea2ff0fb9031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ccddc8e-6343-5b87-aa99-4a041586cf69" :question/math-academy-id "q-86058" :question/problem "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ the vertical line $x=3,$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/47/47b612a3e08ff24055cbffa7d53fb337f1905ec861abd3a8e535088d383162cb.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/19/1927874f5eca0c18b7b61b37d0e6fac5198ec9dcf89205763e88570287330292.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(\\sqrt[3]{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-\\sqrt[3]{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}-6{y}^{1/3}+9)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3{y}^{5/3}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{9{y}^{4/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+9y{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{3}{5}-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+(9-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
